# SLIDE 6 — FEASIBILITY, TECH, SCALE, VALIDATION

**PURPOSE:** Convince the assessor a 6-person team can actually build this, with justified technology and measurable outcomes. Carries Criteria 3, 5, 6, 8.

**TEMPLATE HEADING:** *Feasibility and Viability* (+ technologies).

**LAYOUT**
```
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| FEASIBILITY & VIABILITY                                      |
|                                                              |
| [ STACK TABLE ]                    [ DATA = FREE ]           |
|  choice | why this one              Landsat, Corona,         |
|                                     Sentinel-2, Bhuvan,      |
|                                     Census/LGD -> Rs 0 data  |
|                                                              |
| [ RISKS -> MITIGATIONS ]           [ SCALE PATH ]            |
|  3 rows                            1 district -> 4 states    |
|                                                              |
| [ VALIDATION / EXPECTED OUTCOMES — labelled "targets" ]      |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**EXACT COPY — stack table (keep the "why", that is what is graded)**

| Layer | Technology | Why this choice |
|---|---|---|
| Historical archive & compute | Google Earth Engine (+ STAC, rasterio, xarray) | 50-year petabyte archive with zero local storage cost; cannot be self-hosted |
| Change detection | LandTrendr / CCDC | Peer-reviewed, standard temporal-segmentation algorithms — not hand-rolled |
| Present-day asset mapping | PyTorch segmentation (U-Net / DeepLab) on Sentinel-2 (10 m) | 10 m is the resolution at which farms/water/homesteads are actually separable |
| Spatial database | PostgreSQL + PostGIS | Native geometry operations for overlap and boundary-conflict detection |
| Map / WebGIS | MapLibre GL + COG tiles + PMTiles | Open-source, no licence lock-in, supports offline packs for low-connectivity blocks |
| Document AI | PaddleOCR / Tesseract + IndicNER | Handles Devanagari / Odia / Telugu forms and rejection orders |
| Decision rules | Declarative JSON rule base | FRA amendments become configuration, not code changes |
| Backend / frontend | FastAPI + React | Python-native (shares the ML stack); component UI for dual-user views |

- Rejected alternatives (one line, shows judgement): `Not ArcGIS — licence cost, cannot be deployed to a Gram Sabha. Not object detection (e.g. YOLO) on Landsat — the task is change over time, not object recognition.`

**EXACT COPY — data**
- `All primary data is free and public: Landsat (1972→), Corona declassified imagery (1967–72), Sentinel-2, ISRO Bhuvan, SRTM/CartoDEM, Census village directory + LGD codes, MoTA progress reports.`
- `Data cost: ₹0. Recurring cost is compute only.`

**EXACT COPY — risks → mitigations (use exactly these three)**

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Cloud cover / missing years in the archive | Dry-season (Nov–Mar) composites; publish valid-observation count per year; never silently interpolate a gap |
| Coarse resolution in the oldest imagery (~79 m in the 1970s) | Claim only patch-scale cultivation and settlement clusters; use Corona (~1.8 m) for the earliest period; state the limit on-screen |
| Per-claim records and rejection orders are not openly available | Start with one district using curated/synthetic data, clearly labelled; document-digitization module is the on-ramp for real records |

- Add a fourth if space allows: `Sensitive geodata → public tier shows village-level aggregates only; parcel-level geometry is role-gated and consent-gated.`

**EXACT COPY — scale path**
- `Phase 1: one district end-to-end (Dindori, MP)`
- `Phase 2: four PS-named states (MP, Tripura, Odisha, Telangana)`
- `Same architecture scales — Earth Engine handles archive compute; tiled COG/PMTiles handles map delivery. No re-architecture required.`

**EXACT COPY — validation & expected outcomes (label them as targets)**
> Header must read: **`Expected outcomes — validation targets, to be measured in the pilot`**
- `Year-of-change accuracy: within ±3 years of hand-labelled reference on ≥70% of a labelled sample`
- `Rejection-reason extraction: ≥80% correct category on a held-out set of orders`
- `Asset segmentation: per-class IoU reported (not a single aggregate accuracy)`
- `Negative control: on continuous dense forest the engine must return "no change detected" — it must never fabricate a change`
- `End-to-end: one claim boundary in → one complete evidence dossier out`

**WHY IT MATTERS:** Assessors punish two things hardest — unjustified technology and invented results. This slide fixes both: every tool has a reason, and every number is openly labelled a *target*, not an achievement.

**MISTAKES TO AVOID:** Do **not** present the accuracy figures as results already achieved. The word **"target"** must be visible.

